  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-26",
    "title": "Eye in the Sky: The Milky Way over Lençóis",
    "explanation": " Explanation:   Beauty is in the eye of the beholder.  And there is much to behold in today's  featured image . The Milky Way shines bright in the sky of  Brazil , over the  white sand dunes and dark lagoons  of  Lençóis Maranhenses National Park . We can see different parts of  the  Milky Way  as it  apparently rotates in the sky every night and throughout the year. This composite image was taken later in the night in July from a  latitude  of approximately 2º south. It shows some prominent Milky Way features, such as dark  dust lanes  and colorful  nebulas . But does the Galaxy really appear  arched like a rainbow  in the sky? No, this is an  artifact  of a picture taken with a wide-angle lens. As the Galaxy was artfully framed by the photographer, it matches the contour of the dark lagoon creating the illusion of a  cosmic eye , half in space and half on the ground. Is this  eye in the sky  looking back at you?   Tomorrow's picture:  what's next?",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Milky_Way_Lencois.jpg?w=2576&h=2276&fit=clip",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-26-eye-in-the-sky-the-milky-way-over-lencois/",
    "credit": " Ignacio Fernández ",
    "alt": "A nocturnal landscape shows the bright arch of the Milky Way over a dark lagoon surrounded by white sand dunes in Lençóis Maranhenses, Brazil.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-26T10:26:08.769478+00:00"
}